<Guide
  lang="en"
  route="insurance"
  title="Blackjack Insurance: Should You Ever Take It?"
  h1="Blackjack insurance: is it ever worth it?"
  description="Should you take blackjack insurance? No: it loses about 7.4% of each insurance bet in a 6-deck game. The math, why even money is the same bet and the exception."
  updated="2026-10-06"
  readMinutes={5}
  faq={faq}
  related={['payout65', 'mistakes', 'counting', 'strategy']}
>
  <div slot="top">
    <div class="tldr"><p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> <strong>don’t take insurance</strong>, and don’t take even money either. Insurance pays 2 to 1, but in a six-deck game the dealer has a ten underneath only about <strong>30.9%</strong> of the time, so you lose about <strong>7.4% of every insurance bet</strong>. The only exception is for card counters at a high true count.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what">What blackjack insurance is</h2>
  <p>When the dealer’s upcard is an ace, you’re offered insurance. You can bet up to half your original bet that the dealer’s hole card is a ten-value card (10, J, Q or K), which would give the dealer blackjack. If it is, insurance pays <strong>2 to 1</strong>; if it isn’t, you lose the insurance bet and the hand plays on.</p>
  <p>The name suggests protection, but insurance is really a separate side bet on a single card. Whether it’s a good bet depends only on how likely that card is to be a ten.</p>

  <h2 id="math">The math: why insurance loses</h2>
  <p>In a six-deck shoe, once you’ve seen the dealer’s ace (and nothing else), 311 cards remain and 96 of them are tens. That’s a probability of <strong>30.87%</strong>. Insurance pays 2 to 1, so it would need the ten to show up more than one time in three just to break even.</p>
  <p>Expected value per unit of insurance: 2 × 0.3087 − 0.6913 = <strong>−0.074</strong>. You lose about 7.4 cents of every dollar you put on insurance, which is far worse than the main game played well.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Decks</th><th>Expected loss on the insurance bet</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 deck</td><td>about 5.9%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6 decks</td><td>about 7.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8 decks</td><td>about 7.5%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Compare that with about 0.36% for the whole game on a good six-deck table with perfect <a href={routes.strategy.en}>basic strategy</a>. Insurance is one of the most expensive bets you can make at the table.</p>

  <h2 id="even-money">Even money is insurance in disguise</h2>
  <p>When you have blackjack and the dealer shows an ace, the dealer usually offers “even money”: a guaranteed 1-to-1 payout right away. It sounds safe, and it is: you always win exactly one bet. But it’s mathematically identical to insuring your blackjack.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Your choice</th><th>What happens</th><th>Average result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Take even money</td><td>Paid 1 bet, always</td><td>1.000 bet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Decline</td><td>Paid 1.5 bets about 69.13% of the time, push otherwise</td><td>about 1.037 bets</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Declining is better by about 0.037 bets each time, roughly 3.7%. You’ll sometimes push, and that can sting, but over many blackjacks declining pays more.</p>

  <h2 id="myths">“But I have a good hand”: the protection myth</h2>
  <p>Many players insure a 20 or a blackjack “to protect it” and never insure a 12. That has it backwards: the insurance bet only cares about the dealer’s hole card, and your hand doesn’t change the chance of a ten. If insurance is a bad bet with 12, it’s the same bad bet with 20.</p>
  <p>At European tables with no hole card, such as in <a href={routes.spain.en}>Spanish casinos</a>, insurance is offered the same way and is equally bad.</p>

  <h2 id="exception">The one exception: card counters</h2>
  <p>Insurance becomes a good bet only when more than one in three of the remaining cards are tens. In a shoe game, that happens around a <strong>Hi-Lo true count of +3 or more</strong>, which is why insurance is one of the first plays <a href={routes.counting.en}>card counters</a> learn. If you aren’t keeping an accurate count, the answer is always no.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Remember:</strong> no side bet or rule makes blackjack profitable for a basic-strategy player. Play with money you can afford to lose and read our <a href={routes.responsible.en}>responsible gambling</a> page if it stops being fun.</p></div>
  <p>Want the right answer to become automatic? Drill it on the free <a href={routes.simulator.en}>practice table</a>, which corrects every decision and tells you why.</p>
</Guide>
